[Proposition 9 (Time-fractional compound Poisson process) of arXiv:2608.17493, 'Integrals of some compound processes'] Let $M\sim CP(\lambda, F)$, $L_f$ be the inverse Bernstein subordinator and  $p_f$ be the probability function of $M\circ L_f$. Let assume Condition I and that $\mathcal{D}_t^f p_f(t,\dif x) = \int_0^\infty p(s,\dif x) \mathcal{D}_t^f l_f(t,s)\dif s\ \forall t,x$. Then, $p_f$ satisfies
	\begin{equation}
		\mathcal{D}_t^f p_f(t,\dif x) = -\lambda(I-C_F) p_f(t,\dif x),\ \ \ t\ge0,\ x\in \mathbb{R},
	\end{equation}
	subject to $p_f(0,\dif x) = \delta_0(\dif x)$.

[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $J\subseteq [n]$, $f:\cube^n\to \Rbb$ and $t\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \Dop_J \Pop_t f = \upe^{-t|J|} \Pop_t \Dop_J f,
\end{equation}
and consequently, for all $k\in \Nbb_{\leq n}$,
\begin{equation}
    \left|\grad^k \Pop_t f\right| \leq \upe^{-kt}\Pop_t \left|\grad^k f\right|.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Def GIIA of arXiv:2607.19511, 'A Conditional Probability Hierarchy for Stochastic Choice\footnote{We thank Samson Abramsk'] A stochastic choice rule $c: {\cal G} \to \Delta(\Omega)$ satisfies \textbf{Generalized Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives} (\textbf{GIIA}) if for every $G, H \in \cal G$, and every $E, F \in \cal F$ with $E, F \subseteq G \cap H$, we have:
\begin{equation} 
c_G(E) \times c_H(F) = c_H(E) \times c_G(F).
\end{equation}

