[Theorem thm:hawkes-identifiability of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] Assume $\lambda_0=\ell=\mu$ and $\alpha>0$. Let
\begin{equation}
F(T)=\mathbb{E}[N_T].
\end{equation}
Then the Hawkes parameters are recovered from $F$ near $T=0$ by
\begin{equation}
\mu=F'(0),
\qquad
\alpha=\frac{F''(0)}{F'(0)},
\qquad
\beta=\frac{F''(0)}{F'(0)}-\frac{F'''(0)}{F''(0)}.

\end{equation}
Thus the first expected-signature coordinate $T\mapsto\inner{\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Sig}^M(X)_{0,T}]}{1}$ locally identifies $(\mu,\alpha,\beta)$.

