[Thm 1 of arXiv:2605.05430, 'Dirichlet problems and exit distributions for the telegraph process and its planar extensi'] For $x\in[a,b]$, it holds that 
\begin{equation}u_0(x)=\frac{c+\lambda\,(x-a)}{c+\lambda\,(b-a)}\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}u_1(x)=\frac{\lambda\,(x-a)}{c+\lambda\,(b-a)}.\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}u(x)=\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{c+2\lambda\,(x-a)}{c+\lambda\,(b-a)}.\end{equation}

[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2606.31528, 'Well-posedness and stationary distribution of free stochastic differential equations'] Assume that Assumptions  \ref{assumption:ass1} and \ref{assumption:ass2} hold. Given $T\in[0,\infty)$, and let $U_t^{(n)}$ be defined in \eqref{eq:Euler-approx-1}. Then we have 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\|U^{(n)}_t-U^{(m)}_t\|_2\to 0
\end{equation}
as $n,m\to \infty.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

