[Pro 11 (One-sided domain of dependence) of arXiv:2609.02523, '\bf Strong Ill-Posedness in critical and subcritical regimes for the Hunter--Saxton equati'] Let $u_{0,1},u_{0,2} \in L^\infty(\mathbb R)\cap\dot H^1(\mathbb R)$, and denote by $u_1,u_2$ be their dissipative
solutions, respectively.  
Assume further that
\begin{equation*}
	  u_{0,1} = u_{0,2} \quad \text{on }(-\infty,a],
\end{equation*}
for some $a\in \mathbb R$. Then, setting $M=\max_i\|u_{0,i}\|_{L^\infty}$, it holds that
\begin{equation}
  u_1(t,x) = u_2(t,x)  \qquad \text{whenever } x \leqslant a-tM.
\end{equation}
If, in addition, $\supp u_{0,i}\subset(-\infty,b_i]$, for some $b_1,b_2\in \mathbb R$, then one has that
\begin{equation}
  \supp \bigl(\partial_xu_2(t,\cdot)-\partial_xu_1(t,\cdot) \bigr)  \subset \left(-\infty,   \max_i\left(b_i + \frac{t^2}{4}\|u_{0,i}'\|_2^2\right) \right],
\end{equation}
for all $t\geqslant 0$.

