[Defn vhypergraphon of arXiv:2609.02682, 'LDP for tensor forms'] Let $\mathcal{W}_{v}$ be the space of all real-valued symmetric functions in $L^1([0,1]^v)$. We will refer to $\mathcal{W}_v$ as the space of \emph{``$v$-hypergraphon"}s, or simply, the space of hypergraphons. 
\\

For a symmetric and zero-diagonal $v$-tensor $Q_n$, we define the corresponding hypergraphon $W_{Q_n}\in \mathcal{W}_v$ by setting
\begin{align}
W_{Q_n}(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_v):=Q_n(i_1,i_2,\ldots,i_v)\quad\text{ if }\,\max\{\lceil nx_r\rceil ,1\}=i_r\text{ for all }r\in   [v]. 
    \end{align}

[Lemma 35 of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Let $C>0$ be a constant,  $\xi\geq0$, and  $\zeta\in[N^{-C},N^C]$. Suppose there exists a constant $q\in[0,1)$ such that for any $\te\in[\zeta, N^C]$, we have the implication
        \begin{align}
                \xi\prec\te \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \xi\prec\te^q\zeta^{1-q}. \nonumber
        \end{align}
        If $\xi\prec N^C$, then $\xi\prec\zeta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma logmoment of arXiv:2608.17031, 'A shape theorem for periodic branching diffusion'] Fix $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $e\in S^{d-1}$. Let $\lambda_e>0$ be the minimizer in (equation eq:prelimCriticalSpeed of the paper), and recall that $\zeta_e = \lambda_e e$. Let $(Y_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the solution of (equation eq:SDETiling2.0 of the paper) under the measure $\mathbb{P}_x^{\zeta_e}$. Let $\hat{Y}_t:=(1/t)e\cdot(Y_t-Y_0)$. For every $\eta \in \mathbb{R}$ and $e\in S^{d-1}$, there exist $\theta_1,\theta_2\in \mathbb{R}$ such that, for every $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\theta_1}{t}\leq \frac{1}{t} \log \expec{x}{\zeta_e}{e^{\eta t \hat{Y}_t}} -\gamma((\lambda_e + \eta)e)+\gamma(\zeta_e) \leq \frac{\theta_2}{t},
    \end{equation}
    where $\gamma$ is the principal eigenvalue introduced in (equation eq:Intro:EigenvalueProblem of the paper).

[Theorem 5 (The factorial formula) of arXiv:2609.03881, 'Eulerian insertion operators and an Eulerian form of the Pieri rule'] In the coordinates \((\delta,u)\), the following identity holds as a
formal power series in \(z\):
\begin{equation*}
 \G(z)
 =\exp\bigl(-\log(1-\delta z)E\bigr).
\end{equation*}
Consequently, for $r\geq 1$, 
\begin{equation}
 \G_r
 =\frac{\delta^r}{r!}E(E+1)\cdots(E+r-1).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, on \(\mathbb Q[x,y]\),
\begin{equation}
 r!\G_r
 =\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}(xyD+j(y-x)),
\end{equation}
where \(y-x\) acts by multiplication.
In particular,  the operators
\(\G_r\) commute pairwise.

