[Theorem thm:sep of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] For $\mathbb{P}_{\vec x}$-almost  every $\mathcal{W}$, there exist $\delta_1(\mathcal{W})$, $\delta_2(\mathcal{W})>0$ such that for any closed subset $\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{W}$ and all $x\in B(0,1/2)\setminus \mathcal{A}$, we have 
    \begin{equation}
\mathbf{P}_x\left(\mathrm{dist}(W(\tau),\mathcal{A})>\delta_1 \,\Big |\,  W[0,\tau]\cap \mathcal{A}=\varnothing\right)>\delta_2,
    \end{equation}
    where $\tau$ is the first hitting time of $\partial B(0,1)$ by $W$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:lacoin of arXiv:2510.24424, 'On the Fourier Coefficients of Critical Gaussian Multiplicative Chaos'] Let $(X_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be defined as in Equation (equation eq:*scale of the paper) and 
\begin{align}
    U(t):= \begin{cases}
        \sqrt{2}t-\frac{\log t}{2\sqrt{2}}+\frac{4\log\log t}{\sqrt{2}} &\quad \text{if } s\geq  e, \\
         \infty &\quad \text{if } s<e.
     \end{cases}
\end{align}
Then as $A\to\infty$,
\[
    \mathbb{P}\bigg(\exists{t\geq 0},\exists{\theta\in [0,1]}, X_t(\theta)>U(t)+A\bigg)\to 0.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

