body:
[Lem thm1zero of arXiv:2605.06488, 'Continuous-state branching processes with L\'evy-Khintchine drift-interaction: Laplace dua'] The process $X^{\mathrm{m}}$ admits a Markovian extension $X^{\mathrm{e}0}$ verifying almost surely,  for all $t\in [0,\infty)$
\begin{equation}
 X_t^{\mathrm{e}0}(x)=X_t^{\mathrm{m}}(x) \text{ for all } x\in (0,\infty] \text{ and }
 X_t^{\mathrm{e}0}(0)=\underset{x\rightarrow 0}{\lim}\!\downarrow  X_t^{\mathrm{m}}(x) \in [0,\infty].
\end{equation}
Assume $\neg \Htwo: \int_1^{\infty}\frac{\mathrm{d} u}{\Sigma(u)}<\infty$ and $\Hone$. If $Y^{\mathrm{e}\infty}$  has its boundary $\infty$ absorbing, equivalently $Y^{\mathrm{e}\infty}=Y^{\mathrm{m}}$ a.s. and $0$ inaccessible then, under  
$0^+\cdot \infty,\ \infty^-\cdot 0,$
\begin{equation}\mathbb{E}_{x}[e^{-X^{\mathrm{e}0}_ty}]=\mathbb{E}^{y}[e^{-xY^{\mathrm{m}}_t}], \ \forall (x,y)\in [0,\infty]^2,\ t \in [0,\infty).\end{equation}
In particular,  $X^{\mathrm{e}0}$ is Feller and its entrance law from $0$, which can be degenerated into $\delta_0$, has a Laplace transform characterized by 
\[\mathbb{E}_{0}[e^{-X^{\mathrm{e}0}_ty}]=\mathbb{P}^{y}(Y^{\mathrm{m}}_t<\infty)=\mathbb{P}^{y}(\tau_\infty^{+}> t), \ \forall (x,y)\in [0,\infty]^2,\ t \in [0,\infty).\]
The process $X^{\mathrm{e}0}$ has thus $0$ non-absorbing (actually entrance) if and only if 
$$\forall y\in (0,\infty),\ \mathbb{P}^{y}(\tau_\infty^{+}> t)>0, \text{ for some } t\in (0,\infty),$$
i.e. the minimal $\mathrm{CBDI}(\hat{\Psi},\Psi)$, $Y^{\mathrm{m}}$, has $\infty$ accessible (and absorbing).

[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2606.14443, 'SEMICLASSICAL LIMIT OF POLYAKOV-LIOUVILLE MEASURE and Q-Curvature Uniformization ON EVEN-D'] The fields \(h_0\) and \(h\) are related by
	\[
	\scalar{h}{\phi}
	\overset{d}{=}
	\scalar{h_0}{\phi}
	+
	\sigma\scalar{\phi}{1} \xi,
	\]
	where $\xi$ is a standard normal random variable independent of $h_0$.

