[Lem lemXetastar of arXiv:2411.14766, 'Axis-Driven Random Walks on $\Z^2$ \\ (transient cases)'] Assume $z=(x,1)$ or $z=(1,x)$, there exists $c_3>0$ such that for large $x$,   
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}_z(\overline{Z}_{\eta})=x+c_3+O(x^{-1/2}) 
. 
\end{align}
similarly $\mathbb{E}_z(|\overline{Z}_{\eta}-x|) \sim c_4$ with $c_4>0$. 

Moreover for any $\beta<1$, there exists $c_5>0$ such that for any large $x$,
\begin{align} 
    \mathbb{E}_z(\overline{Z}_{\eta}^{\beta}) = x^{\beta}+c_5x^{\beta-1}+O(x^{\beta-3/2}).    
\end{align}

[Lemma EX_formula of arXiv:2607.22480, 'Distinguishability threshold for random geometric graphs'] Recalling the definition of $A_s$ from the statement of Claim~(claim:Z_s_manipulation of the paper), we have
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E} X\!=\!A_s\!+\!\frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}\!\sum_{s<i<j\leq n} \!\!\!\! \lambda_{si}\lambda_{sj}\lambda_{ij}+\frac{1}{d}\!\!\!\!\sum_{s<i<j\leq n} \!\!\!\! \lambda_{si}\lambda_{sj}\lambda_{ij}(z_p-\lambda_{ij}) \!\sum_{s<r\leq n}\!\!\lambda_{ir}\lambda_{jr} +\widetilde O\Big(\frac{n^3p^2}{d^{3/2}}\Big).
\end{align}

