[Definition 7 (Bounded support condition) of arXiv:2608.29872, 'Local Laws and Outlier Eigenvalues for Spiked Separable Covariance Matrices'] A random matrix $X$ is said to satisfy the {\it bounded support condition} with parameter $q$ if
\begin{equation}
\max_{i,\mu}\abs{x_{i\mu}} \prec q, 
\end{equation}
Here $q$ is a deterministic parameter, typically satisfying \(n^{-1/2} \le q \le n^{-c_\phi}\) for some small constant \(c_\phi>0\). Whenever \eqref{eq_support} holds we say that $X$ has support $q$. In particular, under the arbitrarily high-moment assumption (\ref{eq_highmoment}) one may take $q=n^{-1/2}$ by Lemma \ref{momentmethod}.

[Lemma 59 of arXiv:2506.10494, 'Geometric Jensen-Shannon Divergence Between Gaussian Measures On Hilbert Space'] Let $\Ncal(m_0,C_0)$, $\Ncal(m,C)$, $\ker(C) = \ker(C_0) = \{0\}$, be two equivalent Gaussian measures on $\Hcal$, with
	$C = C_0^{1/2}(I-S)C_0^{1/2}$, where $S \in \SymHS(\Hcal)_{< I}$. Then
	\begin{align}
		||C^{-1/2}x|| = ||(I-S)^{-1/2}C_0^{-1/2}x||, \;\; \forall x \in \range(C^{1/2}) = \range(C_0^{1/2}).
	\end{align}

