[Lemma fiberwise-ind-kappa-completion of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\kappa$ be $(S,T)$-regular, and let ${\mathcal{C}}$ be a $T$-$\infty$-category with $\kappa$-small $S$-colimits. Then ${\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa}\circ{\mathcal{C}}$ is $S$-cocomplete, and the inclusion ${\mathcal{C}}\hookrightarrow {\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa}\circ{\mathcal{C}}$ preserves $\kappa$-small $S$-colimits.
    \begin{proof}
        It is clear that ${\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa}\circ{\mathcal{C}}$ is fiberwise cocomplete and that the inclusion preserves fiberwise $\kappa$-small colimits. Moreover, for every $s\colon A\to B$ in $S$, the restriction $\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa {\mathcal{C}}(B)\to\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa{\mathcal{C}}(A)$ admits a left adjoint, and the inclusion satisfies the Beck--Chevalley condition with respect to this adjoints.

        We now again apply [descent-lim]*{Theorem~B} to see that for any $s'\colon X\to C$ in $\PSh(S)$ such that $X$ is a $\kappa$-small colimit $\mathop{\textup{colim}}_{I}X_\bullet$ of representables, the restriction ${\mathcal{C}}(B)\to ({\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa}\circ{\mathcal{C}})(X)$ admits a left adjoint; beware that the target will be typically different from $\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa({\mathcal{C}}(X))$. We now claim that the square
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                {\mathcal{C}}(X)\arrow[r,hook]\arrow[from=d,"s'^*"] & ({\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa}\circ{\mathcal{C}})(X)\arrow[from=d,"s'^*"']\\
                {\mathcal{C}}(C)\arrow[r,hook] & ({\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa}\circ{\mathcal{C}})(C)
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        is vertically left adjointable. As the horizontal maps are fully faithful, this amounts to saying that $s_!'\circ{\mathop{\textup{incl}}}$ factors through the bottom inclusion. Applying the description of $s_!'$ from [descent-lim] again shows that $s_!'\circ{\mathop{\textup{incl}}}$ sends a $y\in{\mathcal{C}}(X)$ with components $y_i\in{\mathcal{C}}(X_i)$ to the colimit of some $I$-indexed diagram $i\mapsto s_{i!}'y_i$. By the above, each $s_{i!}'y_i$ is contained the image of the bottom inclusion; the claim follows as the latter is closed under $\kappa$-small colimits.

        To complete the proof, it then only remains to show that the Beck--Chevalley map $s'_!f'^*\to f^*s_!$ of functors $\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa{\mathcal{C}}(A)\to\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa{\mathcal{C}}(C)$ is invertible for any pullback $((diag:pb-regular-card of the paper))$. As both source and target preserve ($\kappa$-filtered) colimits, it suffices to check this after precomposing with ${\mathcal{C}}(A)\hookrightarrow\mathop{\textup{Ind}}\nolimits_\kappa{\mathcal{C}}(A)$. Using that Beck--Chevalley compose, this follows from the previous paragraph and the assumption that ${\mathcal{C}}$ have $\kappa$-small $S$-colimits.
    \end{proof}

[Definition 8 of arXiv:2605.02002, 'Glauber dynamics for random field Ising models on bounded degree graphs and MLSI'] Consider $\mu$ a probability distribution on $\Omega$. Then a localization scheme having $\mu$ as target distribution is made up of a pair of continuous time processes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The noising process $(X_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$. This is a Markov process such that $X_0\sim\mu$ and $X_1$ follows a Dirac measure.

    \item A denoising process $(Y_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$. This process is defined as the reversal of $(X_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$: $Y_\theta=X_{1-\theta}$. Thus $Y_0$ is a Dirac measure and $Y_1\sim\mu$. 
\end{itemize}
Since $X_t$ is a time reversal of $Y_t$, we use the denoising process $(Y_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$ to represent the localization scheme and call it the localization process.

