body:
[Defn Abstract provability structure (APS) of arXiv:2608.29914, 'Fixed points in abstract provability structures'] A structure $S = \langle L, \leq, \top, \bot, \Box, \boxtimes\rangle$ is called an \emph{abstract provability structure} (APS) if the following conditions hold: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $L$ is a nonempty set.
        \item $\leq$ is a partial order on $L$.
        \item $\top$ and $\bot$ are elements of $L$.
        \item $\Box$ and $\boxtimes$ are unary operations on $L$ satisfying the  following conditions for all $x, y \in L$:
        \begin{description}
            \item[(C1)] If $x \leq y$, then $\Box x \leq \Box y$ and $\boxtimes y \leq \boxtimes x$;
            \item[(C2)] $\top \leq \boxtimes \bot$;
            \item[(C3)] If $x \leq \Box y$ and $x \leq \boxtimes y$, then $x \leq \boxtimes \top$;
            \item[(C4)] $\boxtimes x \leq \Box \boxtimes x$.
        \end{description}
    \end{enumerate}

