{"artifact_digest":"sha256:fed32c7a33ac56d88119f84e39decebe6ab06eedc587438ad945d0c0eb448c20","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/fe/fed32c7a33ac56d88119f84e39decebe6ab06eedc587438ad945d0c0eb448c20.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:bc3a7e16e089452d5239a013c8f57d4b416c294db4e378adbebfae66b744a653","bundle_entry_key":"2608-09465-lemma-9-binom","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_075","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_075.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-09465-lemma-9-binom","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: This lemma follows from a direct application of Stirling's formula and Taylor expansion, and we provide it for completeness. Write $p=1/2+k/n$. By Stirling's formula,
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{2^n}{n\choose{n/2+k}}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2\pi np(1-p)}}\exp\left\{-n(\log 2-h(p))\right\}\left(1+\mathcal{O}(1/n)\right),
\end{align}
where $h$ is the binary entropy function in (equation bi-entropy of the paper). 
Since $h$ is symmetric with respect to $p=1/2$, its Taylor expansion at $p=1/2$ contains even terms only: 
\begin{align*}
