[Lemma 61 ($\Delta_0$-Separation) of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] If $A(x,y_0,\cdots,y_{n-1})$ is a $\Delta_0$-formula with all free variables expressed, and $s,t_0,\cdots,t_{n-1}\in \bfL(u)_\ttX$, then 
    \begin{equation*}
        H[\ttX]\Vdash \exists y\in \ttL(u)_\ttOmega \bigl[ \forall x\in y \bigl(x\in s\land A(x,t_0,\cdots,t_{n-1})\bigr) \land \forall x\in s \bigl(\lnot A(x,t_0,\cdots,t_{n-1})\lor x\in y\bigr)\bigr].
    \end{equation*}

[Lemma lem:asep-seed-linear of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The seed functions (equation eq:asep-seed-def of the paper) satisfy the
semi-discrete linear problem
(equation eq:sc-linear-1 of the paper)--(equation eq:sc-linear-j of the paper) and its adjoint
(equation eq:sc-adjoint-1 of the paper)--(equation eq:sc-adjoint-j of the paper) with the data
(equation eq:asep-edge-weights of the paper).  Explicitly:
\begin{align}
\partial_t\psi_{t,x,m}
&=-\gamma\psi_{t,x-1,m}+\gamma\psi_{t,x,m},
 \\
\psi_{t,x-1,m+1}
&=-\tau\psi_{t,x-1,m}+\tau\psi_{t,x,m},

\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\partial_t\phi_{t,x-1,m}
&=-\gamma\phi_{t,x-1,m}+\gamma\phi_{t,x,m},
 \\
\phi_{t,x-1,m}
&=-\tau\phi_{t,x-1,m+1}
+\tau\phi_{t,x-2,m+1}.

\end{align}

[Definition 15 (Transition times) of arXiv:2506.09462, 'Transition Path Theory For L\'{e}vy-Type Processes: SDE Representation and Statistics'] The sets $\mathcal{R}_{A \to B}$ and $\mathcal{R}_{B \to A}$ of transition times are given by
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            \mathcal{R}_{A \to B} = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{Z}_+} (\tau_{A,n}^-, \tau_{B,n}^+) \subset \mathbb{R}, \\
            \mathcal{R}_{B \to A} = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{Z}_+} (\tau_{B,n}^-, \tau_{A,n}^+) \subset \mathbb{R}.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}

[Theorem 3 (Exact CNN representation of the directional classifiers) of arXiv:2609.00402, '2609.00402'] Let \(T\geq2\), \(B=T-1\), $\bm L=(1,\ldots,1)$, $\bm k=\{k_{h,1}=h+1:h=1,\ldots,T-1\}$, $\bm{\mathcal P}=
\{(\operatorname{Pool}_{h,1},w_{h,1},s_{h,1})
=(\operatorname{Id},1,1):h=1,\ldots,T-1\}$, $\bm C=
\{C_{h,0}=1,C_{h,1}=1:h=1,\ldots,T-1\}$, $\bm m=(1,\ldots,1)$. For every \(\lambda>0\), there exist $f_\lambda^+,f_\lambda^-
\in\mathcal F_{\mathrm{CNN}}
(T,B,\bm L,\bm k,\bm{\mathcal P},\bm C,\bm m)$ such that, for every \(\bm X\in\mathbb R^T\), $\mathbb I\{f_\lambda^+(\bm X)>0\}
=\mathbb I\{D^+(\bm X)>\lambda\}$, $\mathbb I\{f_\lambda^-(\bm X)>0\} =\mathbb I\{D^-(\bm X)>\lambda\}$. Consequently, $h_{D^+,\lambda},h_{D^-,\lambda}
\in\mathcal H_{\mathrm{CNN}}
(T,B,\bm L,\bm k,\bm{\mathcal P},\bm C,\bm m)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:2G of arXiv:2411.16572, 'Optimal decay of eigenvector overlap for non-Hermitian random matrices'] Fix small constants $\epsilon,\tau>0$. Then, for any deterministic matrices $\|A_i\| \lesssim 1$ and vectors $\|{\bm x}\|, \|{\bm y}\| \lesssim 1$, the following isotropic local law: 
	\begin{equation}
		\Big| \big\langle {\bm x}, \big(G^{z_1}(\mathrm{i} \eta_1)A_1G^{z_2}(\mathrm{i} \eta_2)-M_{12}^{A_1} \big) {\bm y} \big \rangle\Big| \prec 
		\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \gamma}\eta_*},
	\end{equation}
 and the averaged local law: 
	\begin{equation}
	\Big|\big\langle \big(G^{z_1}(\mathrm{i} \eta_1) A_1 G^{z_2}(\mathrm{i} \eta_2)-M_{12}^{A_1}\big)A_2 \big\rangle\Big|\prec
	\frac{1}{\sqrt{n\ell}\gamma }\wedge \frac{1}{n \eta_*^2},
\end{equation}
hold uniformly for any $ |z_i|\leq 1+\tau$ and $\ell \geq n^{-1+\epsilon}$ with $\eta_*:=\min_{i=1,2}|\eta_i|$, $\ell:=\min_{i=1,2}\rho_i|\eta_i|$, and the deterministic matrix $M^{A}_{12}=M^A_{12}(\mathrm{i}\eta_1,z_1,\mathrm{i} \eta_2,z_2)$ is defined in ((eq:defM12 of the paper)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:converge2general of arXiv:2411.08295, 'Improving the convergence of Markov chains via permutations and projections'] Let $\pi$ be the discrete uniform distribution, $P \in \mathcal{S}(\pi)$ and $Q \in \mathcal{Q}$ be a permutation matrix. Let $\Pi$ be the matrix where each row equals to $\pi$. We have
	\begin{align*}
		D^{\pi}_{KL}(\overline{P}(Q) \| \Pi) &\leq  D^{\pi}_{KL}(P  \| \overline{P}(Q) ) +  D^{\pi}_{KL}(\overline{P}(Q) \| \Pi) = D^{\pi}_{KL}(P \| \Pi),
	\end{align*}
	and the equality holds if and only if $\overline{P}(Q) = P$. 

	Similarly, if $P$ is further assumed to be $\pi$-reversible, then
	\begin{align*}
		c_{KL}(\overline{P}(Q)) &\leq c_{KL}(P).
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:main-vector-exact-fourier-dimension of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] Assume that the dyadic vector-weight law is energy-admissible. Let \(\mu\) be the limiting dyadic vector-weight cascade measure on \([0,1]\), and set \(M=\mu([0,1])\). Then, almost surely on \(\{M>0\}\),
\begin{equation}
\dim_{\mathrm F}(\mu)=\dim_{\mathrm E}(\mu)=\dim_2(\mu)=D_E(X).
\end{equation}
In particular, if \(D_E(X)>0\), then for every \(0<\sigma<D_E(X)\)
there is a finite random constant \(C_\sigma\) such that
\begin{equation}
\lvert\widehat{\mu}(\xi)\rvert
\le
C_\sigma \lvert\xi\rvert^{-\sigma/2}
\end{equation}
for all sufficiently large 
\(\lvert\xi\rvert\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Defn eq:slope_ineq1 of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] We define a partial relation $\prec$ on (finite) fine slopes generated by the following inequalities:
    \begin{equation}
        s^\heartsuit \prec t^\diamondsuit
    \end{equation}
    whenever $s < t$ and $\heartsuit, \diamondsuit \in \{\natural, \flat, \sharp, \dagger\}$, and
    \begin{equation}
        s^\flat \prec s^\dagger \prec s^\sharp, \qquad \text{and} \qquad s^\dagger \prec s^\dagger.
    \end{equation}

[Lemma tail_prob of arXiv:2608.12056, 'Deviation inequalities for U-processes of growing order, with applications to half-space d'] Let $\{X_t, \ t\in T\}$ be the homogeneous Rademacher chaos of order $j$. Then for any $u > 0$,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb P\left(|X_t| \geq ue\|X_t\|_2\right) \leq e^{-u^{2/j}}\,.
\end{align*}

