[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.30349, 'Attenuated Poisson Dirichlet approximations for divisibility configurations'] Let $J_n$ be uniformly distributed on $\{1,\dots,n\}$ and let
\begin{align*}
P_1(J_n) \ge P_2(J_n) \ge \cdots \ge P_{K_n}(J_n)
\end{align*}
denote the prime factors of $J_n$, counted with multiplicity and arranged in decreasing order, where $K_n$ is the number of prime factors of $J_n$ counted with multiplicity. Define the random measure
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{X}_n
:=
\sum_{k=1}^{K_n}\delta_{\log P_k(J_n)/\log n}.
\end{align*}
Then $\mathcal{X}_n$ converges in law to a Poisson-Dirichlet distribution. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

