[Lemma 63 (Young-type inequality) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] Fix integers $d > n,m > 0$ such that $n+m > d$. Then there 
exists a constant $C>0$, independent of $k$, such that 
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\substack{k_1,k_2\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}\\k_1+k_2 = k}}
 \frac{1}{\norm{k_1}^n\norm{k_2}^m}
 \leqs \frac{C}{\norm{k}^{n+m-d}}
\end{equation} 
for all $k\in\Z^d$.

