[Definition 10 of arXiv:2609.01362, '{Double cluster swapping for spin models: \\Pfaffian relations and sharpness}'] We define the function $\I : \R \times \R \to \R \cup \left\{ \infty \right\} $ given by 
\begin{equation}
	\I(x,y) = 
\begin{cases}
	P(x+y) + P(x-y) - 2 P(x) -  2 P(y) \quad & \text{ if } |x| + |y| \leq A,\\ 
	\infty \quad & \text{ otherwise,}
\end{cases}
\end{equation} 
so that{\hypersetup{linkcolor=black}\footnote{The choice to write the measure relative to $\d\l_2 \otimes \d\l_2$ rather than $\d\l \otimes \d\l$ is arbitrary for now but will be useful later on.}}
\[
	(\d\l \otimes \d\l)(\vp,\vp') = \exp( -\I(\vpp,\vpm)) (\d\l_2 \otimes \d\l_2)(\vpp,\vpm).
\]

[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2605.31471, 'Convergence Rates of Continuous-Time Random Walks to Time-Fractional Diffusions with Unbou'] Under Assumption \ref{asm:heavy-tail}, the following estimate holds
        \begin{equation}
            \int_0^\infty | P(\Phi^h_t > a) - P(\hat\Sigma_t > a)| dt \leq (1 + a + a^{-1}) C(\beta) h^{\chi(\beta)},
        \end{equation} 
        where $a>0$, and $C(\beta) > 0, \, \chi(\beta) \in (0, 1)$ can be found explicitly. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 49 (Factor capture and positive exhaustion) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] One has
\begin{equation}
 0\le\widehat N_q\le QR^2\Id.

\end{equation}
Applying the top-eigenspace rule to
$\widehat E_q=R_q^*\widehat N_qR_q$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{a,q}\norm{P_{\widehat V_*}r_{a,q}}^2
 \ge
 \widehat\kappa_{Q,d}
 \frac{\widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A,r)}{R^2},
 \qquad
 \boxed{\widehat\kappa_{Q,d}=\frac1{dQ^2}}.

\end{equation}
At extraction level $J$, stop when
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A,r)
 \le
 \widehat\delta_J
 :=
 \frac{dQ^2R^4}{J}.

\end{equation}
Fewer than $J$ promotions occur, and along the nested trajectory
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{j\ge0}
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A^{[j]},r^{[j]})
 \le dQ^2R^4,
 \qquad
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A^{[J]},r^{[J]})
 \longrightarrow0.

\end{equation}
Consequently every proper residual--residual raw contraction and every
active--residual raw contraction tends to zero, while residual covariance is retained.

[Proposition prop:positive_parity_implies_signed_monotonicity of arXiv:2605.15401, 'Noise Tradeoffs, Stationary Information Flow, and Structural Balance in Unit-Birth Network'] Assume that for each ordered pair $j\neq i$ such that $f_i$ depends nontrivially on $x_j$, the map $x_j\mapsto f_i(x_{-i})$ is globally either nondecreasing or nonincreasing. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every cycle in the underlying signed graph, obtained by ignoring edge orientation, has positive parity, that is, contains an even number of negative edges;

    \item the signed interaction graph is structurally balanced;
    \item there exists $\sigma\in\{-1,1\}^N$ such that
    Assumption~(ass:signed_monotonicity of the paper) holds.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, every structurally balanced interaction graph yields a
signature $\sigma$ for which (equation eq:signed_monotonicity of the paper) holds. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Def: elementary differential trees of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] For $\sigma\in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n,\mathbb{R}^n\otimes (\mathbb{R}^d)^\ast)$ we inductively define the \emph{elementary differential} $\Upsilon:{\mathcal T}\to C( \mathbb{R}^n;\mathbb{R}^n)$ by imposing that, for all $z\in\mathbb{R}^n$, 
		$[\Upsilon_{\bullet_j}(z)]^i:=\sigma^i_k(z)$, $i=1,\ldots,n$, $j=1,\ldots,d$, and, for all $\tau_1,\ldots, \tau_k\in{\mathcal T}$,
		\begin{align}
			&[\Upsilon_{[\tau_1\cdots \tau_k]_j}(z)]^i:=\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_k=1}^n \frac{\partial^k\sigma^i_j(z)}{\partial z_{i_1}
				\cdots \partial z_{i_k}} \, [\Upsilon_{\tau_1}(z)]^{i_1}\cdots [\Upsilon_{\tau_k}(z)]^{i_k}\,.
		\end{align}
		We extend it to a map $\Upsilon:{\mathcal F}\to C( \mathbb{R}^n;\mathbb{R}^n)$ as a morphism with respect to the forest product such that $\Upsilon_\one=1$.

