body:
[Def eq: poincar\'e of arXiv:2412.04918, 'On one dimensional weighted Poincar\'e inequalities for Global Sensitivity Analysis'] A probability measure $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(a,b)$ satisfies a weighted Poincar\'e inequality with weight function $w \in \mathcal{W}(a,b)$ and constant $C>0$ if for every function $f\in H^1(\mu,w)$ such that $\int_a^b f \, d\mu =0$ (we say that $f$ is centered), it is true that
    \begin{equation}

    \scaleobj{1.0}{
        \int_{a}^b f^2 \, d\mu 
\leq C\int_a^b w \, (f')^2 \, d\mu .}
\end{equation}
The Poincar\'e constant, denoted $C_P(\mu,w)$, is the optimal (\textit{i.e.}, the smallest) constant $C$ for which ((eq: poincar\'e of the paper)) holds. If there exists some non-null function which realizes the equality in ((eq: poincar\'e of the paper)), we say that it saturates the weighted Poincar\'e inequality.

