[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2604.18427, 'Expected perimeter and area of the convex hull of planar Brownian motion stopped upon exit'] Define the maximum horizontal displacement of the standard planar Brownian motion $\bfW=(\bfW_t)_{t \geq 0} = (X_t, Y_t)_{t \geq 0}$ at the time it first exits from the unit disk by
\begin{equation}
M = \sup_{0 \leq t \leq \tau_\bbD} X_t.
\end{equation}
We have that the cumulative distribution function of $M$ is given by
	\begin{equation}
		\bbP(M < a) = \frac{2\arcsin a}{\pi - \arccos a}, \qquad 0<a<1.
	\end{equation}
	Furthermore,
	\begin{equation}
		\bbE[M] = \int_0^{\pi/2}\left(1 - \frac{2t}{\frac{\pi}{2}+t}\right)\cos t\D{t} \approx 0.511655.
	\end{equation}
	Consequently, the expected perimeter of the convex hull of $\bfW$ when it first exits $\bbD$ is
	\begin{equation}
		\bbE[\sfP_{\tau_{\bbD}}] = 2\pi \,\bbE[M] \approx 3.214826.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma LemmaUe-24 of arXiv:2601.15223, 'Large time behaviour for a class of 2D and 3D stochastic non-Newtonian fluids of different'] Assume that condition (equation third-grade-paremeters-res of the paper) and Hypothesis (Hyp-f-U of the paper) are satisfied. Then, for every $\mathfrak{r}\in \mathbb{R}$, $\omega\in \Omega$ and $B=\{B(\mathfrak{r},\omega):\mathfrak{r}\in\mathbb{R},\; \omega\in \Omega\}\in \mathfrak{D}$, there exists $\widehat{\mathcal{T}}=\widehat{\mathcal{T}}(\mathfrak{r},\omega,B)>0$ such that for all $t\geq \widehat{\mathcal{T}}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}_{\mathfrak{r}-t}\in B(\mathfrak{r}-t,\vartheta_{-t}\omega)$, the solution $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}(\cdot)$ of system (equation CTGF of the paper) with $\omega$ replaced by $\vartheta_{-\mathfrak{r}}\omega$ satisfies
	\begin{align}
		& \int_{\mathfrak{r}-t}^{\mathfrak{r}}e^{-\int^{\mathfrak{r}}_{\zeta}\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\frac{\sigma^2}{2}-2\sigma y(\vartheta_{\upeta-\mathfrak{r}}\omega)\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)\/\mathrm{d}\/ \upeta}\|\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}(\zeta;\mathfrak{r}-t,\vartheta_{-\mathfrak{r}}\omega,\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}_{\mathfrak{r}-t})\|^2_{2}\/\mathrm{d}\/\zeta
		  < + \infty,
	\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
	& \int_{\mathfrak{r}-t}^{\mathfrak{r}}e^{-\int^{\mathfrak{r}}_{\zeta}\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\frac{\sigma^2}{2}-2\sigma y(\vartheta_{\upeta-\mathfrak{r}}\omega)\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)\/\mathrm{d}\/ \upeta} \Upsilon^{-2}(\zeta,\vartheta_{-\mathfrak{r}}\omega) \|\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}(\zeta;\mathfrak{r}-t,\vartheta_{-\mathfrak{r}}\omega,\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Z}}_{\mathfrak{r}-t})\|^4_{2}\/\mathrm{d}\/\zeta
	  < + \infty.
\end{align}

