[Proposition 87 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Under Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A} and \ref{Assumption_B}, the density of the equilibrium measure can be computed for $h \in [H]$ through
\begin{equation}
 \forall x \in (\hat{a}'_{h},\hat{b}'_{h})\qquad\tan\big(\pi\theta_{h,h}\,\mu_h(x)\big)=\frac{ q^{-}_h(x^-) - q^{-}_h(x^+)}{2\ii \, q^+_h(x)}.
\end{equation}
For $x$ inside a band, the density is also given by
\begin{equation}

1-\exp\big(2\ii\pi \theta_{h,h}\, \mu_h(x)\big)=\frac{2\, q^{-}_h(x^+)}{q^{+}_h(x)+q^{-}_h(x^+)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 7 (Inverse-sine envelope) of arXiv:2609.01570, 'A Proof of Fraenkel's Conjecture'] For integers \(Q\ge2\) and \(d\) with \(1\le d\le Q/2\), put
\(C_0=2\pi-4\).  Then
\begin{equation}
 \frac1d\le K_Q(d)
 <\frac1d+C_0\frac d{Q^2}
 <\frac1d+\frac{16d}{7Q^2}.
\end{equation}

[Deff def:mild of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Fix numbers $r \in [2,\infty), \theta \in \mathbb{R}$, functions $F: B \to \mathcal{B}_{-1+\theta, r}$, $G: B \to \gamma(\mathcal{U},\mathcal{B}_{-1/2+\theta, r})$, a stopping time $\tau$, and assume $x \in L^p(\Omega;C([0,T];B))$.  
     A process $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ to (equation eq:gefx of the paper) up to time $\tau$




    if there is some $\epsilon > 0$ such that for all $T > 0$
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^{T \wedge \tau} \|F(x_t)\|_{-1+\theta + \epsilon, r} + \|G(x_t)\|_{-1/2+\theta +\epsilon}^2dt\Big] < \infty \,,
    \end{equation}
    (see (fractional-domains-deff of the paper) for the definition of the norms)  
    and furthermore
    \begin{equation}
        x_t = S(t)x_0 +(S*F(x))_t + (S \diamond G(x))_t
    \end{equation}
    almost surely on the event $\{t \leq \tau\}$.

[Definition 9 of arXiv:2609.02385, 'Kinetic Fokker--Planck Equations with Drifts in a Supercritical Range'] Let $Q\subset\mR^{1+2d}$ be a bounded domain. A function $u\in\sV_Q\cap\mL_Q^\infty$ is called a weak sub-solution of PDE \eqref{kineticpde} in $Q$ if for any nonnegative $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(Q)$,
\begin{align}
-\int_Q u\p_t\varphi\le\int_Q u\Delta_v \varphi-\int_Q u(v\cdot\nn_x\varphi)+\int_Q(b\cdot\nn_v u)\varphi+\int_Q f\varphi.
\end{align}
We say that $u$ is a weak solution to \eqref{kineticpde} in $Q$ if \eqref{weaksubsolineq} holds with equality for every $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(Q)$. If $u\in\mL_{\rm loc}^\infty\cap\sV_{\rm loc}$ is a weak solution in every bounded domain $Q$, then we call $u$ a global weak solution.

[Corollary 15 of arXiv:2606.16083, 'Eyring--Kramers asymptotics for infinite-dimensional stochastic gradient systems'] Let $0<\ell<\pi$. Then
\[
   \liminf_{\beta\to\infty}\lambda_{\beta,1}^{(\mathrm{CH})}>0 .
\]
Furthermore, there exists $\epsilon_0>0$ such that, for
$\pi<\ell<\pi+\epsilon_0$,
\[
   \liminf_{\beta\to\infty}\lambda_{\beta,2}^{(\mathrm{CH})}>0 ,
\]
and, as $\beta\to\infty$,
\[
   \lambda_{\beta,1}^{(\mathrm{CH})}
   =
   \frac1\pi
   \frac{(\ell^2-\pi^2)\pi^2}{\ell^4}
   \sqrt{
      \frac{\det[\nabla^2\widetilde{F}(x_1)]}
           {|\det[\nabla^2\widetilde{F}(x_0)]|}
   }\,
   e^{-\beta(\widetilde{F}(x_0)-\widetilde{F}(x_1))}
   \left(1+O(\beta^{-1/2})\right).
\]

