{"artifact_digest":"sha256:514ceba7ea140251878f658ababbf27c99d3f33bfb901ed01d8b182365bd4c4a","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/51/514ceba7ea140251878f658ababbf27c99d3f33bfb901ed01d8b182365bd4c4a.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:ec0ad18e01209c93ec03a3b579fe739318dac7e458a78c28052e3c0e9f79abb8","bundle_entry_key":"2411-15724-lemma-15-lem3-3","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_109","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_109.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2411-15724-lem-15-lem3-3","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The case $\eta < 0$ is obvious. It suffices to consider the case $\eta \geq 0$. Notice that for $\varepsilon \in (0, 1]$,
$$
g_\eta(\varepsilon/4) =  2^\eta \text{\rm{e}}^{-\varepsilon} + 2^\eta g_\eta(\varepsilon),
$$
so that
$$
 \text{\rm{e}}^{-1} 2^\eta + 2^\eta g_\eta(\varepsilon) \leq g_\eta(\varepsilon/4) \leq 2^\eta + 2^\eta g_\eta(\varepsilon).
