[Theorem thm:gamarnik_giant of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (thm:giants_MCMs of the paper), assume further:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If a pair $(i,j)$ is BAD, then $\mathbf d_{(i,j)}(n)$ and $\mathbf d_{(j,i)}(n)$ are null sequences for all $n \geq 1$, i.e., they only have zero entries.
        \item  Considering the natural offspring matrix $M$ on $S^2 \times S^2$ where the $((i_1,j_1),(i_2,j_2))$-th entry is the number $\Exp{D_{(i_1,j_1,j_2)}}\1_{\{j_1=i_2\}}$, there exists a $p\geq 1$ such that all entries of $M^p$ are strictly positive.
    \end{itemize}
    Then if $\eta<1$, $\frac{|\mathcal C^{(1)}_n|}{n}\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow}} 1-\eta$, while if $\eta=1$, then $\frac{|\mathcal C^{(1)}_n|}{n}\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow}} 0$. Additionally, if $\eta<1$, the second largest component $\mathcal C^{(2)}_n$ satisfies $|\mathcal C^{(2)}_n|=O(\log n)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Given two algebras $(A,tr)$ and $(B,tr)$, the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $A,B$ are independent inside their tensor product $A\otimes B$.

\item $A,B$ are free inside their free product $A*B$.
\end{enumerate}

[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2605.27212, '2605.27212'] For every fixed \(M<\infty\) there is a constant
\(C_{p,\varepsilon,M}<\infty\) such that the following holds.  If
\(h\le\varepsilon r\) and
\begin{equation}
    t_*=C_{p,\varepsilon,M}r\log r\,(h+M\log r),
\end{equation}
then, uniformly in \(x\in\Omega\) and all \(t\ge t_*\),
\begin{equation}
    \Prob_x(X_t\notin\Good_{\mathrm H})\le r^{-M}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, if \(L\le r^3\) and \(M=10\), then uniformly in \(x\) and
\(s\ge t_*\),
\begin{equation}
    \Prob_x(\exists\,u\in\{0,1,\ldots,L\}:X_{s+u}\notin\Good_{\mathrm H})=o(1).
\end{equation}

[Lemma 28 (Uniform index separation) of arXiv:2608.06523, 'On the Gardner Transition in the Ising Pure $p$-Spin Glass II'] If $p\geq4$ and
\begin{equation}
 0<m\leq\frac1{p+1},

\end{equation}
then every stationary marginal point satisfies
\begin{equation}
 m^2\xi'(q)>2(p-1)\log2+\frac12.

\end{equation}

