[Definition $\ba$-admissible orientations of arXiv:2509.06273, 'Normalized Matching Property for Competing Urn Models'] For any  \. $\ba=(\mathrm{a}_1,\ldots, \mathrm{a}_d) \in \mathbb N^d$\., an 
 orientation $O$ of $G$ is 
\emph{\color{darkblue} $\ba$-admissible} if 
\[ \outdeg_O(v_i)=\indeg_O(v_i)=\mathrm{a}_i \quad   \text{ for all }  i \in [d].\]
For $S \subseteq X$, define 
\begin{align*}
	\aM_{G,\ba}(S) \ := \ 
		\begin{array}{l}
		\text{number of $\ba$-admissible orientations $O$ of $G$ with  }\\[4pt]
		\text{\. $\Out_O(v_n)=\{e_j \. \mid \. j \in S\}$ \ and \  $\In_O(v_n)=\{e_j \. \mid \. j \in X\setminus S$\}.} 
	\end{array} 
\end{align*}
By construction, \hskip.03cm $\aM_{G,\ba}$ \hskip.03cm is a balanced weight function for vertices of $H_X$.

[Theorem inv th2Pre of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Let $h \in L_{\mathbb{F}}^2 (0, T ; H^1 (G^{\prime} ) )$ and $R \in C^{1,3}(\overline{Q})$ satisfy

\begin{align}
    |R(t, x)| \neq 0 \quad \text { for all }(t, x) \in[0, T] \times \overline{G}.
\end{align}

Assume that $ g= a_{3} y $, where $a_{3} \in L^{\infty}_{\mathbb{F}}(0,T; W^{2, \infty}(G))$.
If
\begin{align*}
    y \in L_{\mathbb{F}}^2 (0, T ; H^2(G) \cap H_0^1(G) )  \cap L_{\mathbb{F}}^2 (\Omega ; C ([0, T] ; H_0^1(G) ) )
\end{align*}
satisfies (system2bu of the paper) and
\begin{align*}
    & \frac{\partial y}{\partial \nu}=0 \quad \text { on }(0, T) \times \partial G, ~ \text{${\mathbb{P}}$-a.s.},
\end{align*}
then
\begin{align*}
h (t, x^{\prime} )=0 & \text { for all } (t, x^{\prime} ) \in(0, T) \times G^{\prime},~ \mbox{${\mathbb{P}}$-a.s.}
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

