body:
[Proposition 41 (Axis formula for a metric-tree translation) of arXiv:2607.26377, '\textbf{Entire Logarithmic Signatures\\ of Bounded-Variation Paths in Finite Dimensions}'] Let $(\mathscr{T},d)$ be a complete metric tree and let $L:\mathscr{T}\to\mathscr{T}$ be a surjective isometry. Suppose that a closed geodesic line $\mathcal{A}\subset\mathscr{T}$ has an isometric parametrisation $\alpha_{\mathcal A}:\R\to\mathcal{A}$ satisfying
\[
L(\alpha_{\mathcal A}(t))=\alpha_{\mathcal A}(t+\tau)\qquad(t\in\R)
\]
for some $\tau>0$. For $x\in\mathscr{T}$, let $q$ be the projection of $x$ onto $\mathcal{A}$. Then $Lq$ is the projection of $Lx$ and
\begin{equation}
[x,Lx]=[x,q]\cup[q,Lq]\cup[Lq,Lx],
\end{equation}
with intersections only at consecutive endpoints. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
d(x,Lx)=2d(x,\mathcal{A})+\tau,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}=\{x\in\mathscr{T}:d(x,Lx)=\tau\}.
\end{equation}
Thus $\mathcal{A}$ is the unique minimum-displacement axis of $L$.

