[Theo 67 of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] Let $Y=(Y_n)_{n\in\mathbb Z}$ be a stationary, irreducible, and aperiodic Markov chain with countable state space $B$, transition matrix $P$, and unique stationary distribution $\pi$.  
Then the following properties are pairwise equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
\item $Y$ is geometrically ergodic;
\begin{comment}
that is, there exists $\rho\in(0,1)$ such that for every $a\in B$,
$\Vert P^n(a,\cdot)-\pi(\cdot)\Vert_{{\scriptscriptstyle \mathrm{TV}}}\le C_a\,\rho^n$.
\end{comment}
\item $Y$ satisfies Gaussian concentration;
\item $Y$ has exponential return times;
\item $Y$ is a finitary process with exponentially decaying coding radius;
\item $Y$ is a coding of an i.i.d. process;
\item $Y$ is finitarily isomorphic to an i.i.d.\ process.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

