{"artifact_digest":"sha256:465dad3a0ae345b1e957849f999b59526215ec5fa9c2b54faaf69b6eaeaeb744","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/46/465dad3a0ae345b1e957849f999b59526215ec5fa9c2b54faaf69b6eaeaeb744.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:99f35281719264e1c5fccbd3a28c9b5558009930d28fda9bcd8b69095259cf63","bundle_entry_key":"2511-10630-lemma-35-tree","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_042","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_042.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2511-10630-lemma-35-tree","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Recall $U$ from (equation def:SingleBall of the paper).
    Observe that it suffices to show $U(i, j) \geqslant \Phi_{\ast}/d$ for each edge $(i, j) \in E$ since this corresponds to permutations $\sigma$ that map $i$ to $j$, in particular, implying that $i$ and $j$ are in the same cycle in the cycle decomposition of $\sigma$.

    We construct the tree $T$ iteratively. We start with a single vertex so that $V = \{1\}$ and $E = \emptyset$. At each step, we add a vertex $j \in V^{\mathsf{c}}$ to $V$ and an edge $(i,j)$ to $E$ satisfying
    \begin{equation*}
        U(i,j) \geqslant \frac{1}{d} \Phi_{\ast},
    \end{equation*}
