[Proposition 36 of arXiv:2606.15425, 'Well-posedness of stochastic parabolic equations with gradient nonlinearities and applicat'] Let $z_0 \in L^0(\Omega;B^{d/q}_{q,2p})$ be $\mathcal F_0$-measurable and take values in a compact subset $\mathcal A \Subset B^{d/q}_{q,2p}$. Given a measurable process $c \colon \Omega \times [0,\tau] \rightarrow \mathcal X_c$, there exists a 
deterministic $t_0 > 0$ and unique mild solution $z$ of 
\begin{equation} 
\d z_t  = \left(\Delta z_t - |\nabla z_t|^2 + \widetilde h(z_t,c_t) + \widetilde\Phi(z_t,c_t) |\nabla z_t|\right) \d t
\end{equation}
with $$z \in L^\infty(\Omega;\mathbb Z_q(\tau_0)),$$ where $\tau_0 = \tau \wedge t_0$. In particular, for $(z,c)$ given by Lemma \ref{lemma:phasefieldlocalstrongex}, $z \in \mathbb Z_q(\tau_0)$. Moreover, if $z_0\geq 0$, $\mathbb P$-a.s., then $z_t \geq 0$ for all $t \in [0,t_0 \wedge \tau)$, $\mathbb P$-a.s.

