body:
[Proposition prop:HWILSU of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on ${\mathbb R}^n$ which satisfies a log-Sobolev inequality as well as $\int x_i d\mu = 0$ for any $i=1, \ldots,n$, and define $f\colon {\mathbb R}^n \to {\mathbb R}$ by $f(x) := x^TAx$, where $A=(a_{ij})_{ij}$ is a symmetric $n \times n$ matrix. Then,
    \[
    \mu(|f - {\mathbb E} f| \ge t) \le 2\exp\Big\{-c \min \Big(\Big(\frac{t}{\sigma^2\lvert A \rvert_{\mathrm{HS}}}\Big)^2, \frac{t}{\sigma^2\lvert A \rvert_{\mathrm{op}}}\Big)\Big\}
    \]
    for any $t \ge 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

