[Definition def:CLTplus of arXiv:2602.08591, 'The Yang--Mills measure on compact surfaces as a \\ universal scaling limit of lattice gau'] Let $s>0$. A family of probability measures $(\mu_t)_{t>0}$ is said to have the property $C(s)$ if 
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item for each $t>0$, $\mu_t$ is ad-invariant and invariant by inversion,
    \item for all $v,w\in \mathfrak{g}$, $\int_G \langle \log(g),v\rangle_{\mathfrak{g}} \langle \log(g),w\rangle_{\mathfrak{g}}  \mu_t(\mathrm{d} g)=t\left\langle v,w \right\rangle_{\mathfrak{g}}  +o(t) $,
    \item $\int_G \vert \log g\vert^{3}\mu_t(\mathrm{d} g)=O(t^{\frac{3}{2}}) $,
    \item For all $t>0$, $\mu_t$ has the following Fourier decay,
    \begin{equation}
\exists C>0; \forall \lambda\in \widehat{G},\forall t>0, \vert \widehat{\mu_t}(\lambda) \vert \leqslant \dim(V_\rho) C\left(1+\sqrt{tc_2(\rho)} \right)^{-s}
\end{equation}
where $c_2(\lambda)$ is the second Casimir number of the representation $\lambda$.
\end{enumerate}

[Lemma 69 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Fix some $K\in \N$ and $0 < t_1 < t_2 < \dots < t_K$. Then for any $\varepsilon'>0$ and initial data $\hfixp_0 \in \UCp$, we have
