[Lemma of arXiv:2607.29020, 'Entropic Symmetrization Resistance'] \label[lemma]{lem:f_- and u are symmetrizers}
Let $G$ be a compact group with Haar measure $\mu$. 
Writing $f_-(\cdot) = f(\cdot^{-1}) \in \mathcal{P}(G)$, and $u = \frac{1}{\mu(G)} \in \mathcal{P}(G)$, we have $\{f_-,u\} \subseteq \Sym(f)$. 

\begin{proof}
We observe that 
\begin{align*}
\Sym = \Sym_L 
&= \{ g \in \mathcal{P}(G) \mid \text{for all $z \in G$, } (g * f)(z) = (g * f)(z^{-1}) \} \\
&= \{ g \in \mathcal{P}(G) \mid \text{for all $z \in G$, } \int_{G} g(w) (f(w^{-1}z) - f(w^{-1}z^{-1}) ) \,d\mu(w) = 0 \} ,
\end{align*}
thus clearly 
$u \in \Sym$. For $f_-$, compute
\begin{align*}
(f_- * f)(z^{-1}) 
&= \int_G f_-(w) f(w^{-1}z^{-1}) \,d\mu(w) \\
&= \int_G f_-(zw)f(w^{-1}) \,d\mu(w) \\
&= \int_G f_-(u)f(u^{-1}z) \,d\mu(u) 
= (f_- * f)(z). \qedhere
\end{align*}
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

