[Lemma Lem:expo_decay of arXiv:2411.13030, 'Classification of the limit shape for 1+1-dimensional FPP'] For any distribution $G$ and non-negative integers $u,v$ there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for all $k$
\[
\mathchoice
{\mathbb P \left( \rule{0pt}{11pt}  T((0,0),(u,v))> k  \right)}
{\mathbb P \left( T((0,0),(u,v))> k \right)}
{\mathbb P \left( T((0,0),(u,v))> k \right)}
{\mathbb P \left( T((0,0),(u,v))> k \right)}
\le C \,2^{-k}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2608.28964, '2608.28964'] For any $g$ satisfying \eqref{eq g}, the weight $\Theta_g$ defined by \eqref{eq weight} extends by zero to a function in $C^\infty([0,1])$ and satisfies
	\begin{equation}
	\partial_r^k\Theta_g(0)=\partial_r^k\Theta_g(1)=0,\quad k\in\N.
	\end{equation}
    Moreover, there exists a constant $C>0$, depending only on $D$, $\omega$ and $\nu$, such that
	\begin{equation}
	|\Theta_g'(r)|^2\leq Cs_g^3\lambda_g^4e^{-2s_g\alpha(x,r)}\xi(x,r)^3,\quad(x,r)\in Q.
