body:
[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.28198, 'Performative Privacy: When Differential Privacy Maximizes Utility'] Given the aggregated empirical mean mechanism, the exact probability that a specific individual data point $z$ is purged (i.e., its log-likelihood ratio breaches the threshold $\tau$) conditioned on $Z_t$ is given in closed form by:
\begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\Lambda(o_t|z) > \tau\mid Z_t)
    =
    1 - \Phi\left(
    \frac{\tau (N_t-1)\gamma}
    {\sqrt{N_t}\|z-\mu_z\|}
    -
    \frac{\sqrt{N_t}\|z-\mu_z\|}
    {2(N_t-1)\gamma}
    \right)
\end{equation*}
where $\Phi(\cdot)$ denotes the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal distribution. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2609.01116, 'Vector-Carleson, Calder\'on, and Poisson--Atomic Criteria for\\ Generalized Hilbert Operat'] $\Pcal_{\phi,p}:\ell^r\to L^r$ is bounded if and only if
$\Ccal_{\phi,p}:H^t\to\ell^t(H^2)$ is bounded, and
\[
 \boxed{\|\Ccal_{\phi,p}\|^2=\|\Pcal_{\phi,p}\|.}
 \tag{4.1}
\]

[Prop eq:B2 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] There exists $p^-_\sigma(\cdot, \cdot,\cdot)\in \mathrm{p} \mathscr{B}((0,\infty))\times \mathscr{B}^*(E)\times \mathscr{B}(E)$ such that 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}^x\left[f(X_t);t\leq \sigma \right]=\int_E p^-_\sigma(t,x,y)f(y)\mu(\mathrm{d} y),\quad \forall t>0,x\in E, f\in \mathrm{b}\mathscr{B}(E). 
    \end{equation}

[Theorem 7 (Spectral domination of root events) of arXiv:2607.13210, 'Punctured Wilson--Evolving Sets and Root Identities for Massive Kirchhoff Forests'] For distinct $x_1,\ldots,x_k$ elements of $V$, let $A=\{x_1,\ldots,x_k\}\subset V$. Then, it comes:
\begin{equation}
  \Pp(A\subset\Root)
  \leq
  \prod_{i=1}^k
  \min\left\{
    \beta_q(x_i),
    \frac{q}{q+\lambda_1(D_{i-1})}
  \right\}.
\end{equation}
In particular, it follows:
\begin{equation}
  \Pp(A\subset\Root)
  \leq\prod_{x\in A}\beta_q(x).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition of arXiv:2609.01926, 'On Sum Ranges for $3n$-convergence'] For two adjacent points $a$ and $b$ with complement $c$, we can define a flip in two directions (when applicable):
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(1)] If the index of $c$ is greater than those of $a$ and $b$, then send $a$ and $b$ to the other unique pair of points $d$ and $e$ such that $c + d + e = 0$. This is called a flip outwards as the indices of $d$ and $e$ are greater than those of $a$ and $b$.
        \item[(2)] If $a$ and $b$ are not on the starting triangle and the index of $c$ is less than those of $a$ and $b$, then send $a$ and $b$ to the other unique pair of points $d$ and $e$ such that $c + d + e = 0$. This is called a flip inwards as the indices of $d$ and $e$ are less than those of $a$ and $b$.
    \end{enumerate}

[Corollary 19 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Users being relayed by a given building.}

Let $(\alpha, \beta)\in \Half$ be a given point such that $\mathcal{L}([\beta,  +\infty[)>0$, and consider the landscape $\tilde{\landsc} := \landsc\cup \{(\alpha, \beta)\}$. 

Then, the expected length of the zone of eventual relaying of $(\alpha, \beta)$ is equal to 
$$\e[\ell(\zone(\alpha, \beta))] =\left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        \displaystyle\int_{0}^{\beta} -\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{\primi(\beta)}{\primi(u)^2}du & \mbox{if } \beta<S \\
        +\infty & \mbox{if }\beta=S,
    \end{array}
\right.$$
where $S$ denotes the supremum of the support of $\mathcal{L}$. 
In particular, under the Palm measure, the average length of the zone of eventual relaying for a typical building is infinite. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemm l:bulk-resampling of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] There exists $C>0$ such that for all sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$ 
    and all $k\in \llbracket 1,M-1\rrbracket$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \limsup_{n\to\infty}
        \frac{1}{n^{2/3}}
        \mathbb{E}_\mathrm{re}\left[
            \left|
                \mathcal{H}^{\xi,(n)}_{{\,\mathrm{TF}_k}}
                -
                \mathcal{H}^{\eta_k,(n)}_{{\,\mathrm{TF}_k}}
            \right|^2
            \1_{\xi,\eta_k\in\mathsf{Good}_{\varepsilon,n}}
        \right]
        \leqslant 
        C\varepsilon^{2 - 3\gamma} \log^{8}(1/\varepsilon).
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition time-independent upperbdd_rho of arXiv:2609.00627, 'Global well-posedness of radially symmetric strong solutions to two-dimensional compressib'] Let $\beta,\gamma$ satisfy (equation TH1. 1-1 of the paper).  Then there is a constant $C>0$, depending only on $\alpha, \mu, \beta, \gamma$, $\Omega$, $\|\rho_0\|_{L^\infty}$, $\|\boldsymbol{u}_0\|_{H^1}$ and $\|\nabla\boldsymbol{d}_0\|_{H^1}$ but not on time $T$,  such that
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{aligned}
			\Vert \rho \Vert_{L^{\infty} L^{\infty}} \le C. 
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}

