body:
[Thm 2 (Explicit Form of the Predictable Compensator) of arXiv:2606.20925, 'Explicit Predictable Compensators for Single Jump Processes with Initial Information'] Let $X$ be the special semimartingale satisfying the integrability conditions introduced above. Its unique predictable compensator is given by $A_t=C(t\wedge\gamma)$, where the $\cH$-measurable function $C(t)$ is explicitly computed as follows.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item For all $t<t_G$, the function $C(t)$ is given by the integral:
        \begin{equation}
            C(t)=\int_{(0, t]}\frac{d\left[F(s)\overline{G}_{\cH}(s)\right] + H(s) dG_{\cH}(s)}{\overline{G}_{\cH}(s-)}
        \end{equation}
        \item Furthermore, if the jump distribution possesses an atom at the right endpoint, i.e., $\PP(\gamma=t_G)>0$, the exact value of the function $C$ at $t_G$ is given by $C(t_G)=C(t_G-)+\Delta C(t_G)$, where the terminal jump is:
        \begin{equation}
            \Delta C(t_G)=H(t_G)-F(t_G-)
        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}

[Theo 2 of arXiv:2512.02766, 'Fine-Graining and Continuous Space Scaling Limit of the $H^{2|2}$ Model on the Hierarchica'] Consider the $H^{2|2}$-model (with boundary condition $\eta$) on a finite graph $\mathcal{G}$ by the data of its random Green function operator $G=H^{-1}$. If $U\subset V$ is a subset of vertices indistinguishable from outside, then one can effectively replace $U$ by a single vertex $u$ and coarse-grain the matrix $G$ into a matrix $G'$ on $l^{2}(V')$ with $V'=(V\setminus U)\cup\{u\}$, whose coefficients are defined by
\begin{equation}
    G'(k,\ell)=\begin{cases} G(k,\ell) & k,\ell\notin U \\ \frac{1}{|U|}\sum_{i\in U}G(k,i) & k\notin U,\ell=u \\ \frac{1}{|U|^2}\sum_{i,j\in U}G(i,j) & k=\ell=u \end{cases}.
\end{equation}

The resulting matrix $G'$ has the same law of the Green operator defined by the $H^{2|2}$-model (with boundary condition $\eta'$) on the reduced graph $\mathcal{G}'=(V',E',W')$ with edge weights
\begin{equation}
    W'_{k,\ell}=\begin{cases}W_{k,\ell} & k,\ell\ne u \\ \sum_{i\in U}W_{k,i} & \ell=u, k\ne u\end{cases}.
\end{equation}
(and boundry condition is $\eta'_{u}= \sum_{i\in U}\eta_{i}$ and $\eta'_{k} = \eta_{k}$ for $k\ne u$).

Furthermore, the inverse of $G'$ is of the form~\eqref{eq:Form_H}, i.e. an $H^{2|2}$-model random Schrödinger operator on the reduced graph $\mathcal{G'}$, where the random vector $\beta'$ is defined by $\beta'_k=\beta_k$ if $k\in V'\setminus\{u\}$ and
\begin{equation}
    \frac{1}{2\beta'_u}=\frac{1}{|U|^2}\sum\limits_{i,j\in U}\widehat{G}^{U}(i,j)
\end{equation}
where $\widehat{G}^{U}=(H_{U,U})^{-1}$ with $H_{U,U}$ is the sub-matrix of $H$ on $U\times U$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 9 (Free independence \cite{NS2006, mingoFreeProbabilityRandom2017}) of arXiv:2606.31528, 'Well-posedness and stationary distribution of free stochastic differential equations'] Subalgebras $\mathscr{A}_1,\dots,\mathscr{A}_m \subset \mathscr{A}$ are said to be \emph{freely independent} if for every $n\in\mathbb{N}$, every choice of indices $i_1,\dots,i_n \in \{1,\dots,m\}$ with $i_k \neq i_{k+1} \text{ for all } k,$
and every choice of operators $X_k \in \mathscr{A}_{i_k}$ satisfying $\tau(X_k)=0$ for all $k=1,\dots,n$, one has
\begin{equation*}
\tau(X_1\cdots X_n)=0.
\end{equation*}

[Theorem thm:7 of arXiv:2412.06086, 'Zeros of conditional Gaussian analytic functions, random sub-unitary matrices and q-series'] Assume that the test function $h (q)$ satisfies the assumptions of Theorem (Thm:M of the paper). Then $G_{n, \tau} [h]$  converges, in the scaling limit $n\to \infty$, $n\tau=t>0$, to
\begin{align}
G_t[h] = 1+ \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (-1)^k \int_0^1 \ldots \int_0^1 \frac{e^{t\left(1- \frac{1}{q_1 \cdots q_k} \right)}}{q_1 \cdots q_k}\, \prod_{j=1}^k \frac{q_1 \cdots q_j}{1- q_1 \cdots q_j} \, dh(q_1) \ldots dh(q_k) \, .
\end{align}

