[Definition def-adm of arXiv:2603.17861, 'Gaussian concentration, integral probability metrics, and coupling functionals\\ for infin'] A probability measure $\mu$ on $(\Omega,\mathfrak{F})$ is said to be asymptotically decoupled if there exist $g:\mathds N\to\mathds N$ and $c:\mathds N\to [0,\infty)$ such that
$\lim_{n\to\infty}g(n)/n=0$, $\lim_{n\to\infty} c(n)/|\ensuremath{\Lambda}_n|=0$, and $\forall j\in\mathds Z^d, \forall n\in\mathds N, \forall A\in\mathfrak{F}_{\ensuremath{\Lambda}_n+j}, \forall B\in \mathfrak{F}_{(\ensuremath{\Lambda}_n+g(n)+j)^{\mathrm{c}}}$,
\begin{equation}
\e^{-c(n)}\mu(A) \mu(B)\leq \mu(A\cap B)\leq \e^{c(n)}\mu(A) \mu(B).
\end{equation}
If only the upper bound holds, one says that $\mu$ is asymptotically decoupled from above.

[Lem meso-ld-bound of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] For every $\varepsilon>0$, there exist $\gd,c>0$, $C<\infty$, and $\ell_0<\infty$ such that, for all $\ell\geqslant\ell_0$ and $f\geqslant1$,
\begin{align}
\E\sum_{v\in
\mathcal{N}_{\left(1/\mathsf{v}^\star+\gd\right)f}^{(\ell)}}
\mathds{1}\left\{
 |\mathsf{p}_\ell(v)|\geqslant f,\ 
 \left|
 \frac{|v|}{f}-\frac{\beta_{\mathsf{c}}}{\theta}
 \right|>\varepsilon
\right\}
\leqslant Ce^{-cf}.
\end{align}

